text:
I was looking for the least expensive big-brand hotel (Marriott, Hilton, etc) to stay in for a night in Philly. This fit the bill. The staff is INCREDIBLE...so polite, helpful, ...really stand-outs.
But the facility is a bit lacking:
- pressure in jacuzzi is non-existent;
- our coffee maker didn't work;
- very tiny towels at the pool and jazuzzi;
- no in-room safes;
- poor soundproofing in rooms.
The soundproofing was a problem because there were a lot of partiers returning home when the bars closed, which woke us up...we were still trying to sleep off jet lag from our incredible trip to Sardenia and Rome! (See my hotel reviews!) Because I had just stayed in two hotels in multiple rooms, I was especially sensitive to the noise...this was really bad. Our rooms in noisy Rome were much quieter!
Other than that, if you're looking for a reasonably priced accommodation in Center City, this place will do the trick. Although beware there is no free breakfast.. try the Reading Terminal Market, a short walk away.

Goods:
The room was nice and clean;
Good location;
Bads:
The staff is friendly, but my feeling was that they need more people in order to keep up with all the gests;
The sofa is not a good choice for a third person, very uncomfortable;
The breakfeast is a nightmare! There is absolutely no room for you and your family to sit down, only a few tables that do not fit even 1/4 of the hotel capacity. The line to get anything is always huge and the food is not tasty at all. The staff carries garbage throughout the breakfeast room while you are still there and that is very annoying.
Conclusion:
Eventhough the location is nice and the rooms are good, they should definetely work on their breakfeast. If they improve in this area, this hotel can become an excelent cost effective choice for families and couples.

cleaned_text:
in my opinion, this is by far the best sushi and japanese joint in town! this place is as authentic as you can get for phoenix, az. my husband and i are addicted to the chirachi bowl which is packed full of rice, seaweed, and piled high with the chef's selection of the freshest raw fish. this incredible bowl of deliciousness is $20! you could not find this much fresh raw fish at a restaurant for this price anywhere else in phoenix. 

if i get the chirachi bowl idea stuck in my head, i have to have it and nothing else will satisfy my craving. speaking of..i think i will order one for take out right now! ;)

i rate sushi ken at the highest rating possible: 5 noms: "nom, nom, nom, nom, & nom!" :d

text:
Check in time was 3pm and got there at noon and they were able to provide me with a room; the accommadations were great; king size bed with added sofa and 40 inch TV; restroom was clean although more towels should have been available; the receptionist and clean up maids were courteous
The bed was comfortable and for a change the pillows were firm but confortable;
I went to my sister 50th wedding anniversary and had never visited the area; the young lady at the front desk drew me a map to get to the Church and even though it was like ten miles away she provided an excellent drawing that I was able to find the location; I wished I could remember her name to thank the hotel for her assistance beyond the call of duty;

cleaned_text:
i adopted one of my dogs from the cchs, and i cannot say enough wonderful things about this facility. i imagine they are working a shoestring budget but they nonetheless take wonderful care of the animals who unfortunately find themselves here. 

they have a rather involved screening process to make sure that the animals they adopt out really do find their proverbial "forever homes." when we adopted, there was a brief waiting period to avoid any impulsive adoptions, as well as a requirement that we bring in our other dog to meet our adoptee to make sure they got along. all of the staff were very friendly and you could tell from the way they interacted with the animals that they really care about all of the animals. personally, i do not think i could do such heartbreaking work and maintain the kind, gracious, and dedicated demeanor that i have observed in the workers here. 

if you are interested in making a donation to the cchs, you can do so here:

if you read my original review you may have read some humorous venting. i am playing nice so i removed the first paragraph :)

located in the main part of chinatown, the same plaza as 99 ranch market but more to the center near mother's korean grill. there is not a big sign but if you are passing by you may run into an elderly asian woman inviting you in. the service and entrees were good, i had no complaints. but you will probably stop by mainly for their desserts. their cold and very tasty shaved snow. besides frost bites shavery which is changing their name to snowflake shavery!? this is probably the only other place in vegas i would highly recommend for shaved snow treats. they have a bunch of combinations of flavors of shaved snow and toppings i had the peanut with boba, matcha with red bean, mango with mango, blue berry with condense milk, taro with taro, and i think mango with mango love them all and more than some. their servings for shaved snow are big and inexpensive. check out the pictures i know you want some now too.

i guess all 13 of us who wrote a review for this business listing do not know anything better.

loved it. 

dinner for my date and i was delicious large pizza with pesto, mozz cheese, generous amounts of sausage and meatball. their large is plenty for two people. it was four average sized slices for each of us. very flavorful! 

for a nice dine-in restaurant inside the pallazo, our dinner only totalled to $23 with tax. amazing food, service and location. i will probably come back every time i visit vegas. :)
